Changes made in this fork, as required by section 4(b) of the Apache License:

  * Pricing is extended with this plugin's official CNY price table, explicit
    peak/off-peak windows, and a Chinese public-holiday calendar
    (src/shared/officialRates.ts). Upstream prices only from the client's
    models.dev USD book with a flat `PEAK_FACTOR = 2` and has no holiday table,
    so its holidays bill at the peak rate. Here the official table takes
    precedence for the models it lists, the host fold resolves each settlement's
    period against it, and other models keep pricing from models.dev.
    `host/config.ts` gains the `holidays` field; `host/foldMetering.ts`,
    `host/activity.ts`, `host/fold.ts`, `host/timeline.ts`, `host/index.ts` and
    `client/cost.ts` thread it through. The `contextTimeline` stateVersion is
    bumped 27 -> 29 and `contextActivity` 4 -> 6 because the folded period
    classification changed: 28/5 introduced the holiday-aware split, and 29/6
    keyed that split on the model rather than the provider route, so an official
    model reached through a third-party proxy bills by the Beijing window instead
    of being booked peak and left undoubled.
  * A composer-dock cost readout is added (src/client/components/composerDock.tsx,
    src/client/dockReading.ts, src/client/styles/composerDock.css): absent
    upstream. `client/index.ts` registers it on `conversation.composer.dock`, and
    `client/i18n.ts` gains its dictionary keys. The collapsed trigger copies the box
    metrics and palette of the official readout pill on the same row (StatsPills'
    `.pill` — the "48.6M tok · 缓存命中 97%" one; NOT ContextMeter's `.trigger`,
    which is a different kind of control and genuinely differs): the 12px pill size
    `calc(var(--dsh-content-font-size-secondary, 13px) - 1px)`, the 20px line box,
    `padding: 1px 8px`, the 999px full capsule with `corner-shape: round`, the 14×14
    frozen glyph, `label-tertiary` rest, `label-secondary` hover and the official
    `separator-primary` separators, none bolded — only peak/off-peak keeps its
    semantic red/green. The period is computed live from the official table rather
    than hardcoded: the trigger carries a per-second countdown to the next flip, and
    the tooltip plus the expanded card spell out the peak windows
    (`shared/officialRates.ts` gains `peakWindowsText`, `msUntilFlip`,
    `allDayOffReason` and `countdownParts`; `client/format.ts` gains `fmtCountdown`
    so the units are localized). Weekends and holidays show the reason instead of a
    countdown. When the row cannot fit the full reading the sentence
    degrades to a clock icon
    (`primitives.ts` gains `IconClock`, resolved from the official icon set) plus
    `fmtCountdownCompact`. The verdict compares the space LEFT for this row (the
    parent's content box minus the official sibling entries, which sit in the same
    row, and the gaps) against the width the full reading needs, supplied by an
    always-rendered, visually hidden measuring probe — never a viewport check (a wide
    window with a narrow column must still degrade) and never the row's own rendered
    width (which would oscillate). The read is injectable through an
    `IsRowTooNarrow` seam on `makeComposerDock` so tests need not stub `window`.
    Its expanded card
    embeds the two
    Summary Statistics cards (`statsTokens`/`statsCost`) rather than drawing its own
    token and period blocks, so the readout and the page share one dataset and one
    layout; its own bars, tints and balance row were removed with them. The card's
    look mirrors the official stat dialog (`stat-dialog.module.css`'s `.panel`, the
    "Token usage" panel the readout pill opens): the `--dsw-specific-menu` surface,
    `--dsw-radius-lg`, `--dsw-elevation-prominent`, z-index 1100, `padding: 16px`
    and 12px/18px text, plus the official `.title`/`.titleRule` head (label-primary
    at 500, ends pinned apart, 8px below, a 0.5px hairline with 10px under it). The
    card is `createPortal`ed to `document.body` for the same reason the official
    panels are: the composer sits in `ConversationRoot`'s `.composerSeat`
    (`position: sticky` with `z-index: 7`), a stacking context in which a
    z-index-1100 card is only ordered locally. Its width is NOT content-driven:
    nearly every block inside is percentage-sized (`.lc-dock-cards > .lc-card
    { width: 100% }`, the tables, the `.lc-sl` auto-fill grid), and percentage
    children contribute no intrinsic width, so a `max-content` card collapses to its
    264px floor and crushes the contents. It uses `min(440px, 100vw - 24px)` — the
    official panel's own cap, and the measured width at which a 7-row legend and the
    4-column rate table both stop wrapping — with a 264px floor and a 560px ceiling,
    clamped to the viewport, so a short trigger cannot clip the
    right-hand figures of those wider cards, and
    no horizontal scrollbar can appear (`overflow-x: hidden` beside `overflow-y: auto`).
  * A cost card is added to the session view (src/client/components/statsCost.tsx,
    src/client/styles/statsCost.css): absent upstream. It sits between the Token
    and Timing cards and reports the session's spend by billed bucket, the
    peak/off-peak split, and the account balance. `client/cost.ts` gains
    `estimateSessionCostByBucket`, which prices the four buckets and the two
    periods in one pass; `estimateSessionCostByPeriod` now reads its period figures
    from it, so every cost surface shares one accumulation.

  * The package is named `dsh-cost-breakdown` (upstream's is `dsh-context`), and its
    icon.svg is this fork's own three-segment "usage meter" gauge rather than
    upstream's document-sheet emblem. Upstream's source files are otherwise
    carried over unmodified; the identity strings the runtime depends on (route
    paths, locale namespace, slot ids) follow the package name.
  * The loader row id in `cordis.patch.yml` is the short handle `cost-breakdown`,
    deliberately distinct from the package name. The Host settings namespace is
    derived from that row id (`dsh-settings` describes each entry with
    `ns: entry.options.id`), so `client/index.ts` declares it separately as
    `SETTINGS_NS` while the locale namespace and the `plugins.bundle.config` seat
    keep the package name; the Plugins page prints the row id and the module name
    on separate lines, so one string for both printed the same line twice.
  * Documentation comments were added across the whole `src/` tree (including the
    CSS sheets): every exported declaration carries a doc block, and non-obvious
    logic carries explanatory inline comments. The comments are written in Chinese.
    This touches most files inherited from upstream, so those files are no longer
    byte-identical to their upstream revisions (see below). Only comments changed —
    no statement, expression, identifier, string literal, type or formatting of
    code was altered, which was enforced mechanically by comparing the pre- and
    post-edit trees token by token. Upstream's behaviour, structure and public
    surface are therefore unchanged; the divergence is documentation only, and a
    rebase should treat these edits as comment noise.

Files carried over from upstream are otherwise unmodified unless the change is
noted above or in the commit history.

`lib/` is committed, so the shipped artifacts were regenerated after the comment
pass. The rebuild is code-equivalent to the previous one: the host bundle is
identical once comments are removed, the declaration file's declarations are
unchanged, and the client bundle's JavaScript and its Tailwind CSS payload are
unchanged. The only substantive bundle difference is that SEVEN DEAD utility
rules (`.collapse`, `.relative`, `.container`, `.border`, `.ring`, `.outline`,
`.transition`) are no longer emitted. They existed only because the older English
comments happened to contain those words, which Tailwind's content scanner reads
as class candidates; nothing in the source used them, and the bundle now has no
dangling `--tw-*` custom-property references as a result. `lib/index.d.ts` gained
the doc blocks that reach it from the source.

Post-pass fix (own code, not upstream): the composer-dock detail card was
positioned from a height measured before the card existed, so an open card sat
at a top computed for a size it never had and could overlap the composer row.
The card now mounts as soon as the readout opens (hidden until placed), the
first measurement happens against the real element, and a ResizeObserver
re-places it when its asynchronous content changes its height. A no-op guard
keeps an unchanged measurement from re-rendering. Covered by a regression test
in tests/client/components/composerDock.spec.ts.

Follow-up: the composer-dock card's surface token was corrected from
`--dsw-alias-bg-layer-1` (the primary card layer) to `--dsw-alias-bg-layer-2`,
which is what DSH's own floating surfaces use (its dialog/float rules pair
layer-2 with a shadow) and what this plugin's other overlays (`.lc-tip`) already
used. In the dark theme layer-1 is bluish-875 (#232324) against layer-2's
bluish-850 (#2c2c2e), so the open card read noticeably darker than the harness's
own popovers; in the light theme both resolve to pure white, so the change has no
effect there.

UI label rename (own code, superseded below): the two entry points are named by what
they show. The account-level page (the left-nav entry and that page's heading) is
"统计" / "Statistics". The `sidebar.guideTitle` key this briefly introduced was
retired once the tab and the card agreed, so the name had one source rather than two
that could drift. Note that a panel keeps the term "上下文" inside itself where it is
accurate ("当前上下文", "上下文趋势"); that is panel vocabulary, not an entry's name.

Token and cost separated per surface (own code): the three surfaces were doing
overlapping jobs and the names followed the jobs. The conversation tab — which shows
context composition, trend, browser, events, file activity, the agent network and the
token card, and no amount whatsoever — is now "上下文" / "Context". The right-side
column is "本会话汇总" / "Session Summary": one token section (the same Token card the
tab and the composer card use, so all three agree) above one spend section. The
left-nav page stays "统计" / "Statistics" and covers EVERY session, its token and spend
sections likewise kept apart. This reverses the previous entry in this file, which had
made all three share "汇总统计" / "Summary Statistics"; that name stopped being true the
moment each surface began answering a different question. `client/sidebar.ts` and
`client/icon.tsx` (`makeContextTabTitle` now takes the key) were changed so the sidebar
can carry its own label. Tokens never appear next to an amount: the agent-network node
cards report token volume only, and the daily chart is a token-only chart.

Per-platform spend is billed in the platform's own currency (own code): each platform
gets its own card carrying its own billing currency and remaining balance, and the
cards are never summed — adding credits to yuan to dollars yields a number that means
nothing. DeepSeek shows ¥, third parties $, credit platforms credits, whatever the UI
locale; this deliberately reverses upstream's "convert everything to the display
currency" behaviour. `formatNativeSpend` in `client/cost.ts` performs the one-way
conversion, `client/platformSpend.ts` tags each row with its currency and with the
models it could not price, and `client/platformBalanceState.ts` is the single balance
poll shared by the statistics page, the session summary and the composer readout.
Per-model rows live inside each platform card rather than in a second table, which had
repeated the card's own figures verbatim.

Test harness fix (own code): `tests/client/setup.ts` installs a localStorage shim when
`globalThis.localStorage` is undefined. Node 26 defines its own `localStorage` accessor
that resolves to undefined without `--localstorage-file`, and it shadows jsdom's, so
every spec that seeded or read a remembered balance failed with "Cannot read properties
of undefined" (82 cases across three files). The shim supplies the platform's storage
shape; no production code was relying on the broken behaviour.
